From 046b5ba9e49f2ac45c95493901a56bfa79e1a92d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:02:13 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 124/171] domainset: read rule sets of the source format in one
 pass, build the trie by radix

The domain lists of a rule set are now one module, common/domainset:

- ReadSource reads a rule set of the source format into what
  json.UnmarshalExtended[option.PlainRuleSetCompat] reads of it, in one
  pass, without a copy of the names. The names of a default rule come as
  its matcher (DomainMatcher, as from a binary rule set); the other
  fields of a rule go to sing-box's own decoding as a small object.
  Input of any other shape (comments, logical rules, keys in other
  letters or twice, values of other shapes) and any error are left to
  sing-box, rule by rule or whole, so the result and the errors are
  sing-box's own. Local and remote rule sets use it.
- Collector keeps a key as where its name is in the text, read from its
  end; only names with escapes or not of ASCII are copied.
- The trie is built a level at a time, the keys of a node parted by
  their next byte (a radix sort in the order of the trie): no sort by
  comparison, no map; repeated names and names that are suffixes too are
  dealt with on the way. Byte for byte domain.NewMatcher's trie.
- Where sing stops the process (an empty suffix, a domain and a suffix
  of the same key) the builder returns an error.

TestReadSourceSameAsStock compares 100000 random rule sets with
sing-box's decoding (37438 of them read here, the rest left to it);
FuzzReadSource ran 22.6 million inputs. A rule set of 100000 varied
names: 390 ms and 277 MB allocated with sing's decoding and matcher,
24 ms and 23 MB here; 300000 names: 870 ms and 862 MB, 130-170 ms and
72 MB.
